course: dictionary
lesson: 14079
title: Measurably: you can see the change
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Measurably» is an adverb. It tells you that something changed by an amount you can measure or clearly notice. «The plant grew measurably in one week.» You could measure the plant at the start and at the end and see the difference.
explain: Measurably does not mean a little. It means clearly and noticeably. «My reading has improved measurably» means the improvement is real and easy to see. Look at the words around it: they often show what was measured or when, like «in one week» or «since September».
explain: Measurably is common in science and in reports, where people show evidence. A good check is to swap in «clearly» and see if the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have the right meaning.
fact: Measurably means by an amount that can be measured or noticed.
fact: «Measurably» is an adverb, so it tells you how something changed or happened.
fact: Measurably means clearly and noticeably, not just a tiny bit.
fact: «Measurably better» and «measurably different» are useful phrases to learn.
fact: The word is built from measurable with -ly added to make an adverb.
exercise: «The plant grew measurably in one week.» What does measurably mean here? | *Clearly, by an amount you can measure | Very slowly, so nobody could see it | Happily, with lots of singing | Upside down
exercise: Ella says measurably just means a bit. Why is she wrong? | It means quickly and loudly | It is a longer word for happy | *It means clearly and noticeably, not just a tiny bit | It means a bit, so Ella is right
exercise: «My reading has improved measurably.» Which word is the best swap for measurably? | quietly | *clearly | barely | slowly
test: «The new bridge is measurably stronger than the old one.» What does measurably tell you? | The bridge is made of wood | The old bridge was stronger | The bridge is painted a new colour | *The difference in strength is clear and can be measured
test: Which sentence uses measurably in a formal, evidence-based way? | My cat sat measurably on the mat | Measurably, I like cake | *The results showed a measurably higher score | The sky looked measurably blue today
exam: «The class tested two seeds, and one grew measurably taller in a week.» What does measurably mean? | Upside down, like a flip | Sadly, with tears | Quietly, so you cannot hear it | *Clearly, by an amount you can measure
